sections.body:
A component's props are typically described with an `interface` (or an equivalent `type`), then referenced as the type of the destructured parameter:

```tsx
interface UserCardProps {
  name: string;
  age: number;
  isOnline?: boolean; // the ? marks this prop as optional
  onSelect: (userId: string) => void;
}

function UserCard({ name, age, isOnline = false, onSelect }: UserCardProps) {
  // ...
}
```

The `?` on `isOnline` marks it optional -- callers may omit it, and a default value can still be supplied via destructuring, as shown. Function-typed props declare their own parameter and return types too: `onSelect: (userId: string) => void` says any function passed for onSelect must accept a string and doesn't need to return anything meaningful -- and any call site passing a function with a different shape (taking a number instead, or returning something that's used as if it mattered) gets flagged immediately, without needing to run the code at all.

